# The slack of the buffer that actually absorbs the layer. One row per layer whose absorber
# resolves to a buffer, carrying that one buffer's slack and nothing about the other two.
#
# The `S[l, A(l)]` term
#   This is the `S[l, A(l)]` term of the model's own inequality. A layer carries three slacks and
#   names one of them as its absorber; every statement of the form "the shares a layer holds are
#   bounded by its slack" means that one. Written out, the term is layers/absorber.sqlc joined to
#   entries/slacks.sqlc on (filing, layer, buffer), and the third key is the whole point: drop it
#   and you are comparing a layer against every buffer it has.
#
# What dropping the buffer key costs
#   Without it, entries/comparable_slack_shares.sqlc and the rule built on it,
#   checks/slack_unit_mismatch.sqlc, which says "a slack is expressed in the unit of the shares it
#   bounds", would examine every slack in the layer. Most of those pairs are a holder against a
#   buffer that absorbs nothing of theirs, where a differing unit is not a defect at all.
#   arithmetic/roster.sqlc names that rule the guard for `shares_bounded`, whose site restricts to
#   the absorber, so the guard would be strictly wider than the site.
#
# An inner join to the absorber term
#   The join to pm.buffer_term is inside layers/absorber.sqlc and is an inner one. A layer whose
#   absorber term no reader's warrant resolves is not here, which is correct: nothing can be said
#   about the slack of a buffer nobody identified.
-- layers/absorber.sqlc joined to entries/slacks.sqlc on the buffer the layer actually names.
SELECT a.filing, a.layer, a.buffer,
       a.taxonomy, a.term, a.the_readers_warrant,
       s.low, s.mode, s.high, s.unit, s.absent, s.sized
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/absorber.sqlc)
) a
JOIN      (
    :compose(entries/slacks.sqlc)
) s USING (filing, layer, buffer)
